# Operator

This is the server that holds a shard of someone's key and collaborates with central to produce partial signatures that central then aggregates into a full signature.

Clients talk directly to operators and send their shards to them.

At registration time the client doesn't have to perform OAuth, the operator trusts the email address sent by the client along with a confirmation from the central (with which the client does perform OAuth).

Later, though, clients can perform OAuth with operators directly in order to request their shards back or tell the operators to erase them.

## Build

Requires [templ](https://github.com/a-h/templ) for HTML template code generation.

```
templ generate
go build -tags=libsecp256k1 -o ./operator
```

## Run

Execute `./operator` with the required environment variables. Use nginx, caddy or any other reverse-proxy normally.

TLS is needed, but try to not use Cloudflare automatic HTTPS as that means Cloudflare can steal the shards of users.

## Environment

| Variable               | Default       | Required                           |
| ---                    | ---           | ---                                |
| `PORT`                 | `5041`        |                                    |
| `SERVICE_URL`          |               | yes (e.g. `https://po.mysite.com`) |
| `DB_PATH`              | `operator.db` |                                    |
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`     |               | yes                                |
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` |               | yes                                |

Create OAuth 2.0 credentials at [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials). Set authorized redirect URI to `{SERVICE_URL}/po/callback/google`.

### Static binary with musl

For fully static deployment (no libc dependency):

```
templ generate
CC=musl-gcc go build -ldflags="-s -w -linkmode external -extldflags '-static'" -tags=libsecp256k1 -o ./operator
```

Requires `musl-gcc` wrapper installed.

### Other OAuth providers

`GITHUB_CLIENT_ID`, `GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET`, `MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID`, `MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET`, `APPLE_CLIENT_ID` are in the codebase, but haven't been tested. Let's first prove this model works with Google only before introducing more complexity.

## Running as a systemd service

This is an example, adapt as it suits your needs:

1. Create a directory at `/opt/pomegranate`.
2. Place the binary at `/opt/pomegranate/operator`.
3. Place the following file at `/etc/systemd/system/pomegranate-operator.service`:

```
[Unit]
Description=Pomegranate Operator
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=root
WorkingDirectory=/opt/pomegranate
ExecStart=/opt/pomegranate/operator
Environment=SERVICE_URL=https://po.mysite.com GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=... GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Then:

```
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable pomegranate-operator
systemctl start pomegranate-operator
```

## Docker

It is known that some people run Pomegranate on Docker. I believe it should be easy for Docker fans to generate a Dockerfile, so we won't provide any here.
